s=>`A few days later another student says: “I love a module my friend takes. I might just drop my registered class and start theirs on Monday. It’s exploration year, after all.” Cian looks at you.

${s.flags.explorationAction==='advice'?'You remember Julie distinguishing future possibilities from registered modules.':s.flags.plannerConsulted?'You remember the six-module build you checked in your practice planner.':'You recognise this is the same dilemma you encountered earlier.'}

The new student is enthusiastic, and you do not want to put them off trying something different. What advice would you give?`

s=>`Saoirse finds you making notes near the student spaces in Lower House. “I’ve an advising meeting too,” she says. “I thought I was meant to arrive with a Major already picked.”

You tell her that you've enjoyed subjects you didn't expect and are thinking about possible directions. She asks what you'd actually like to know. You begin a list: which subjects you might explore next, whether anything must be taken early to preserve a Major option, how a Minor might fit, and who can resolve the missing module. ${s.flags.prepRecord?'Because you checked your timetable, you can name the specific issue without guessing.':'You realise you have not yet checked the exact modules appearing in your current timetable.'}

Saoirse nods. “So I can go in with questions, not a final answer.” You agree. But the list is getting long. Which question should lead the meeting?`

s=>`Julie welcomes you and asks how the first weeks are going. There isn't a test or a requirement to announce your future Major. This is a conversation about where you are now and what needs checking.

${s.flags.prepRecord?'You open your practice timetable and can point to the modules currently listed. Julie is able to distinguish a possible registration issue from a question about your academic direction.':'You mention a module you think is missing, but cannot remember the code or whether you were actually registered. Julie suggests checking the current timetable and planner together before treating it as a confirmed problem.'}

${s.flags.prepInterests?'You can explain the subjects you enjoyed, what surprised you, and the two directions you might want to preserve. Julie asks about prerequisites and what you might need to investigate before the next advising point.':'When asked about your interests, you struggle to put them into words. Julie gives you space to think and suggests making a short list after the meeting. You have not missed your chance to explore.'}

${s.flags.prepQuestion==='change'?'You ask whether you can swap modules now. Julie explains that an interest or an advising discussion does not change formal registration; any request has to be checked through the approved programme process.':s.flags.prepQuestion==='pathways'?'You ask how to keep two routes viable. Julie suggests checking the relevant early modules, gateways and conditions, rather than promising either route will remain available.':s.flags.prepQuestion==='role'?'You ask which questions belong to an adviser. Julie describes academic pathway guidance and explains where registration and administrative questions need to go.':'Julie asks which issue feels most urgent and helps you turn a general worry into questions to investigate.'}

Before you leave, Julie asks what you will do next.`

s=>`You put three items in your phone notes. First: verify the modules currently recorded for you, and ask the appropriate team if something is wrong. Second: check the requirements or early sequences for possible Majors, rather than assuming every option will remain available. Third: note the decisions and deadlines you need to return to at the next advising checkpoint.

${s.flags.prepRecord&&s.flags.prepInterests?'Because you brought your current build and academic interests into the conversation, the follow-up tasks feel specific and manageable.':'You realise that next time it would help to arrive with your current timetable and a short list of questions. The adviser can still support you; preparation simply makes the time more productive.'}

You haven't made a final Major decision, and you haven't changed a registered module. You have left with a clearer plan and know how to seek further help.`

s=>`For a moment, you imagine standing in Grangegorman on graduation day. Someone asks what your first year was like. You try to picture an answer, but there are too many things that might happen between now and then.

${s.flags.past==='school'?'You remember having a full school day planned for you. University may expect more decisions about how and when you study.':s.flags.past==='other'?'You have previous experiences of managing your time independently. University may still introduce unfamiliar rules and assessment routines.':'Your experience gives you a mixture of habits and expectations. You may need to figure out what transfers well.'}

This is a possible future, not a prediction. The screen slips back to your first morning at TU Dublin.`

s=>`${s.flags.welcomeConcern==='subjects'?'“Paul said you had a question about Music,” Julie says. “What drew you to it in the first place?” You tell her you wanted to try a subject you’d never had a chance to study. She asks what else you’d like to keep open.':'Julie waves you over. “Before we get into modules, what were you hoping Arts would give you a chance to try?” You name one subject, then add another that seems quite different. “Can I do both?”'}

“You can explore more than one area in Stage 1. Let’s look at what has to fit into a four-year, 240 ECTS degree. There’s an approved Major, 30 credits of common Spine modules and 50 credits of Subject Area Requirements. A Minor takes 40 credits if you choose to take one. Any room for Free Electives depends on the rest of the degree you build.”

She pulls up an example. “We can move the pieces around first. Then we can talk about what you’d actually like me to record.”`

s=>`You have explored the arithmetic of an example degree. ${s.flags.degreeMajor===120?'A 120 ECTS Major plus a 40 ECTS Minor would use all 240 credits alongside the required General Education components. There would be no separate Free Elective space. Viability would still require formal checking.':'There may be credit space for further modules, depending on your approved Major, any Minor and the General Education requirements.'}

You ask whether you have to declare a Major now. “Not now,” Julie says. “Stage 1 is exploratory, but some paths have early prerequisites, eligibility checks or capacity constraints. You will submit an indicative pathway preference during Stage 1, while formal Major confirmation comes later in Stage 2.”

You leave knowing that exploration is about discovering academic directions, within an organised degree.`

s=>`You investigate the workload guide. Credits represent total expected learning effort, which includes time in class, reading, projects, practice, assessment and independent study. At TU Dublin, a 5 ECTS module represents an estimated 100 hours of total student effort, including classes, reading, practice, independent study and assessment. Assessment is included in those hours, not added on top; it is not five class hours.

${s.flags.moduleAssumption==='contact'?'You now see why a timetable with gaps is not necessarily a light academic workload.':'The learning hours make more sense once you separate scheduled contact from independent study.'}

You open a sample module descriptor. It lists learning outcomes and assessment components. You realise that studying at university means organising the time between classes, not waiting to be given something to do each day.`

s=>`A timetable notification arrives. A seminar has moved, but that does not automatically mean your registered modules have changed. A friend says you can attend any class that interests you. You remember that academic curiosity and module registration are separate things.

${s.flags.studyPlan?'You also have a study block in your calendar. It is not a guarantee that everything will go smoothly, but it gives you time to begin.':'You still have not decided when to work on the upcoming assignment. The choice will come back when the deadline approaches.'}

The game phone contains a practice timetable and a Curriculum Planner. Neither changes your actual TU Dublin record.`

s=>`${s.flags.swapResponse==='turnup'?'Cian later discovers that attending a class is not the same as being formally registered on its module.':'You and Cian investigate what the words on the programme guidance actually mean.'}

Stage 1 allows academic exploration and opportunities to keep future Major options open. It does not permit unilateral changes to registered modules. Changes may be possible through authorised processes, subject to timetable, capacity, prerequisites, suitability and other requirements.

On your way out you notice a societies noticeboard and a student event. The semester also holds possibilities beyond administrative questions.`

s=>`It is Week 6 and an assignment is due. You have a brief, a marking rubric and a date on the module page. You also have your first experience of deciding how to work without someone checking every day.

${s.flags.studyPlan?'You recognise the study time you set aside earlier, but the assignment still needs care.':'You recall postponing your study plan. Now the deadline is closer than you expected.'}

A memory of your earlier learning appears: at school or in a previous course, someone often reminded you what came next. Here the lecturer expects you to read the requirements and manage the work. Which action would help most?`

s=>`${s.flags.assessmentStrategy==='plan'?'You align your work with the learning outcomes and marking criteria and submit it through the specified system.':'You submit the assignment, but discover that the marking criteria cover elements you did not fully address.'}

A module can use continuous assessment, examinations, practical work, projects or combinations approved in its descriptor. Marks from different assessment elements contribute according to the approved assessment scheme, and some elements may have special pass requirements. Feedback is intended to help you learn what is working and what needs improvement.

Your practice result appears. It is below the pass standard in this fictional scenario. You feel disappointed. What do you do next?`

s=>`${s.flags.failureResponse==='check'?'You investigate what the assessment result actually means.':'You discover that a disappointing mark needs checking rather than an assumption about the entire year.'}

Failing one assessment and failing a module are not always the same thing. Your module's approved assessment scheme, pass requirements and TU Dublin assessment regulations determine the result and what happens next. Depending on the case, reassessment or other provisions may apply, but they must never be assumed or guaranteed by the game. Formal progression decisions are also governed by university rules.

You seek feedback and make a concrete plan to improve your next submission. A short flash-forward shows you working on a later project with more confidence. You do not know the outcome of the real future, but you can see how responding to feedback may matter.`

s=>`${s.flags.meetingPrepared?'You show Julie your current modules, your ranked interests and two specific questions. The conversation quickly turns to the requirements of possible pathways.':'Julie helps you work through your interests and suggests opening the current planner together. You can still have a productive meeting without having everything organised in advance.'}

You talk about a possible Major and an optional Minor. Julie distinguishes an indicative Stage 1 preference from formal Major confirmation later in Stage 2. She explains why certain pathways need earlier preparation and why availability and timetable constraints still apply.

At the end, she asks what you will do after the meeting.`

s=>`${s.flags.transfer==='informed'?'You find a way to preserve curiosity while checking the academic and administrative conditions. Cian can see how the university systems and people work together.':'Cian asks whether the advice would actually work. You reconsider: academic exploration can be encouraged, but official registration and pathway rules still matter.'}

Four years flash forward. You imagine returning to Grangegorman for graduation. You remember some of the subjects you never expected to enjoy, the friends you made, the setbacks that taught you to ask better questions and the advisers and staff who helped along the way. It is one possible future, not a promise.

Looking back, what would you tell the student who arrived on the first day?`

s=>`${s.flags.routeAssessApproach==='assume'?'“An automatic resit isn’t something I can promise from a single mark,” the lecturer explains.':'The lecturer is pleased Saoirse brought the actual brief and feedback.'}

She shows you how the assessment components and their weights are described in the module information. “This isn’t the same as a final module result. We can talk about improving the next piece, but official decisions about reassessment and progression follow the current university rules.”

She suggests visiting the library’s learning support resources to develop a concrete plan.`

s=>{
 const w=s.flags.workStyle==='unplanned'?'Cian mentions an upcoming submission. You realise the blank days you treated as completely free are also useful study time.':s.flags.workStyle==='plan'?'Your early decision to look at module workload helps as submissions approach. There are still things to juggle, but your plan is a starting point.':'You remember checking whether your timetable was correct. Now you need to decide how to use the remaining free time.';
 const t=s.flags.changeRead?'You also checked a published timetable change instead of relying on an old screenshot.':'A new timetable notification reminds you to check the latest published version.';
 const p=s.flags.pathway==='checked'?'You have already flagged the early gateway question for possible future Majors.':'A possible Major comes up again. You have not yet checked whether an early sequence matters.';
 return `<p>${w}</p><div class="scene-note">${p}</div><p>${t}</p><p>Cian asks, “What’s one thing you wish somebody had told us on the first day?”`}
